editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:37:"This morning there's a tenth." He kept his eyes on her face, which hung over him upside down and grey. "It wants a real thing done to me for a long time, at a cost, by somebody who means it, and you had told me at Oxhollow what the long reading was: both hands, and all of you, for minutes. I knew when I lay down what it was likely to be. At the end there was a place in me standing open, and the thing that you read with was at the door. I could have kept that door shut. I'm better at shutting them than at anything else I do. I opened it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:153:"And here's the second. You wrote that the real reason was that you didn't know what I'd say. I know what I'd have said. I knew it by the fourth line of that leaf." Her eyes were level and tired, and there was no heat in them at all. "It was mine to say, last night, through a door, and you took the saying. So I'm keeping the answer. You'll not have it today, and you'll not have it when you're old. You can carry that with the other ten."

