@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+# Movement 006 cold-read review
+
+**Read status:** Fresh-context simulated cold read of the seven supplied chapters, 38–44, in order. This is an editorial simulation using two reader lenses, not a real audience measurement or demographic study. I had no canon packet, prior chapters, plan, or drafting history. The movement reaches a deliberate departure and next-quest turn, but the supplied text is not publication-complete because three `TIER-B` insertions and one `[UNBOUND]` token remain unresolved.
+
+## Unscored reader response
+
+Interest caught immediately in Chapter 38 when Cael says, “I opened it.” The fantastical mechanism is interesting, but the stronger hook is ethical: he knowingly used Oryn’s work without her consent. Oryn’s “You turned my table round” makes the violation intelligible without reducing either character to a position in an argument. Her decision to keep the answer he denied her the chance to give is a precise, painful consequence.
+
+Interest rose again when the new Tide-adjacent fragment became useful rather than merely collectible. Brom’s forge analogy, the lagging ground, and Lira’s “Wait for the ground” turn an inward sensory change into visible training and a real efficiency gain. The birthday meal then supplies earned warmth without pretending the breach is settled. The Quieting investigation catches in a different way: apples teach Karis intellectual restraint, and that lesson becomes method rather than decoration.
+
+Interest slipped most in Chapter 41’s chronology and across the middle of the research sequence. Chapter 41 moves from noon on day 104 back to the night of day 103, then forward again. The signals are present, but the reversal made me stop and reconstruct the order. Chapters 41–43 also restate Karis’s refusal to promote inference into finding several times. The repetition is thematically sound, but eventually I was admiring the method more than feeling the mystery advance. The unresolved production tokens caused the only complete breaks in immersion.
+
+Oryn matters most to me in this movement. Her ethics, appetite for knowledge, refusal to forgive cheaply, care for Lira, gift of the mule, and lonely road afterward all belong to one recognizable person. Lira is close behind: paying for the birthday one-handed and learning to shoot from stillness make her adaptation emotionally legible.
+
+I expect the crew to leave Thornwater, use the broker’s money to locate another Quieting, and discover that the western registry has begun moving in response to the report. I also expect Cael’s three self-readings and eventual report to Oryn to expose the unmoving thing inside him without resolving it cleanly.
+
+I would continue. The strongest reason is not the abstract mystery but the way knowledge now has interpersonal costs: who may inspect whom, who gets told first, and who is already keeping records in the west.
+
+## Two reader lenses
+
+Scores are editorial judgments. A 5 means understandable but inconsistent, 7 means engaging with located weaknesses, and 9 means compelling with few substantial distractions. The two lenses are not averaged.
+
+| Dimension | Exact passage location | Fluent thirteen-year-old reader | Adult genre reader |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Opening pull | Ch. 38, opening table confession through “I opened it” (line 37), then Oryn’s “You turned my table round” (line 109) | **8/10, high confidence.** The secrecy, tenth power, and immediate accusation give a strong problem, though nine named fragments and several past locations arrive before a new reader can sort them. | **9/10, high confidence.** The consent problem converts exposition into confrontation, and the physical intimacy of the examination sharpens it. |
+| Keep reading | Ch. 38, “It was mine to say” (line 153); Ch. 40, the unmoving place all ten currents avoid (around line 347); Ch. 44, “Tomorrow makes three” (line 217) | **8/10, high confidence.** Several questions remain alive and each chapter pays something off, especially the power training and shooting. The research run delays the outward next move. | **9/10, high confidence.** Ethical, bodily, institutional, and archaeological pressures converge without collapsing into one answer. |
+| Interest / freshness | Ch. 39, Cael hearing his fragments as grass, millstone, wick, bellows, nail, glass, silence, and sea; Ch. 41, Karis applying the apple lesson to one archaeological site | **9/10, high confidence.** Powers become sensory and trainable, while research is made concrete with barley, bark, rubbings, and apples. | **9/10, high confidence.** The movement’s unusual center is disciplined observation rather than revelation, and character habits generate the method. |
+| Clarity / flow | Ch. 41, noon on day 104 (line 65) followed later by the night of day 103 (line 125); all four unresolved insertions | **6/10, high confidence.** Scene-level action is clear, but the backward date jump, dense proper nouns, and literal editorial tokens create avoidable rereading. | **7/10, high confidence.** Causal logic is strong, but chronology briefly tangles and the repeated distinction among observation, finding, hypothesis, and fear slows the line. |
+| Character attachment | Ch. 40, Lira’s “It’s still your day” (line 63) and the nineteenth birthday; Ch. 43, Oryn finding the eighth mend has taken (line 135); Ch. 44, Oryn repeatedly reaching for the absent cheek-strap | **9/10, high confidence.** Care is shown through actions and limits rather than speeches, and each crew member contributes differently. | **10/10, high confidence.** Oryn’s anger and care coexist without cancellation; Lira’s pride, Brom’s plain loyalty, Karis’s rigor, and Seln’s restraint remain sharply individual. |
+| Humor / warmth | Ch. 40, the three-versus-two apple dispute, Seln dividing the goat, incompatible birthday songs, and Karis eating the sour keeper | **9/10, high confidence.** The jokes are easy to follow and give relief after the table confrontation. | **9/10, high confidence.** Humor emerges from exact character differences and strengthens the found-family texture without erasing consequences. |
+| Action / suspense | Ch. 39, eleven Wind leans while the ground arrives late; Chs. 42–43, Lira learning the yard bow and taking one knee shot at sixty paces | **7/10, medium-high confidence.** There is no full combat set piece, but the drills have rules, failures, adaptation, and visible wins. | **8/10, high confidence.** Bodily suspense is exact, and the western report supplies quiet external threat. The developed training earns its length. |
+| Progression payoff | Ch. 39, “Wait for the ground” and six clean leans at half the former cost; Ch. 43, Lira’s five-of-nine result at sixty paces; Ch. 41–43, Karis converting error into better method | **9/10, high confidence.** Improvement is countable and comes from trying, failing, and adjusting. | **9/10, high confidence.** Progression operates physically, intellectually, and ethically; capability changes consequence rather than merely raising a statistic. |
+| Connection | Ch. 40, birthday entry tying current warmth to earlier deprivation; Ch. 42, five trial rows becoming one loss; Ch. 44, the crew combining the findings with the clerk’s westbound report | **8/10, medium confidence.** Recurring objects and numbers reward attention, though some references depend on unsupplied history. | **9/10, high confidence.** The book, sling, mule, apple, route arithmetic, registry iron, and “low side of nothing” all carry state and relationship forward. |
+| Read-aloud quality | Ch. 38, Oryn’s accusation; Ch. 44, Brom’s “I was never once afraid of you” (line 123); final “one pace…on the low side of nothing at all” (line 303) | **8/10, high confidence.** Dialogue voices are distinct and most cadences land cleanly. Some long analytical paragraphs and missing insertions interrupt oral momentum. | **9/10, high confidence.** Repetition, parallel clauses, and concrete verbs give the prose authority; the final Oryn sequence is especially controlled. |
+
+## Continuity, limits, and disclosure check
+
+### Canon
+
+No editorial canon evidence was supplied, so I cannot verify canon contradictions. Any concern below is about the internal evidence of these seven chapters only. I found no apparent contradiction that rises above a clarity question.
+
+### Physical state
+
+Physical state is unusually well carried. Lira’s left arm remains tied and unused; the eighth mend is slower than the other seven; her shin may run while the foot must still land flat; and her crossbow technique is built around those exact constraints. Karis’s damaged voice improves by stages but is still restricted. Brom’s string marks harden as expected. Cael’s forearm and ribs cease to dominate once their bills are nearly paid. Oryn’s ordinary fatigue and expenditure remain distinct from loss of her Tide craft.
+
+The only practical concern is reader load, not state logic: Lira’s arm, shin, ankle/foot, sling, and shooting stance are all tracked at once. The repeated healer checks keep them coherent, but a cold reader may briefly treat “the bone may run” as permission for the whole leg until Oryn immediately narrows it.
+
+### Power limits
+
+The movement establishes usable limits rather than convenient ones. Cael acquires a partial fragment only after sustained, costly, directed use with shared stakes; Oryn retains her ability; the new read begins as contact/self at arm’s length; it reveals current and cost but not instant explanation; and its first use destabilizes timing. Wind improves through sensory feedback and practice, not a free amplification. Inside the Quieting, five sigils fail at the same marked edge while Cael’s fragments do not. That exceptional result is stated as observation, not explained.
+
